<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lords of the Articles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century–1690</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lords of the Articles was a standing committee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-scotland">Parliament of Scotland</a>, drawn from its estates, which prepared the legislation that the full parliament then approved. It appeared in the later fourteenth century and under the Stuart kings became a means of royal control over parliament. It was abolished in 1690, after which the Scottish Parliament became much more independent until the union of 1707.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lords-of-the-articles.jpg" alt="Lords of the Articles" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Scotland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
